WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
LARRY ARNN: The stakes are the
stakes of human freedom, which

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.203
depends upon a view
of human nature

00:00:07.203 --> 00:00:09.120
and the government that's
appropriate to that.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:19.510
The greatest political science
started with the classics

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.100
when they began to
study the regimes,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.680
and the worst of all the
kinds of regimes is tyranny.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.880
The rule is in the
interest of the ruler

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:30.150
and that means that the
others are held by force.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.759
They're in greater or
lesser states of rebellion

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:33.780
all the time.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.270
In the 20th century, a new
word was invented for tyranny.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.430
They called it totalitarianism.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.310
In the Soviet Union,
what happens, right?

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:47.700
Your kids have to go to school.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.850
If they don't, you're arrested.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.070
But then what have
they taught in school?

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:56.340
They're taught to inform on you.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.560
People live in terror
of their own children

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.710
because the children are young
and they can't and so they

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:07.260
go to school and they
hear authoritative things.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.220
And you'd be afraid to
tell the children not

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.070
to believe what's in
school because how can you

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:17.320
trust a 10-year-old not
to repeat what you said,

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.920
and to the extent that you can
teach them things like that,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.600
you are depriving them
of their childhood.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:26.940
That's totalitarianism.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.830
I don't myself think that it's
really different in principle

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:36.810
than ancient tyranny, which is
also at war with human nature.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.730
The classic books
raised the question,

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:41.490
how can a tyrant
perpetuate himself,

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:43.615
because they don't last
very long, these tyrannies.

00:01:43.615 --> 00:01:46.710
People don't like them,
and a famous story

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.260
repeated by Aristotle
that they do it

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:56.100
by lopping off everything
tall, everything excellent,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.920
everything superior.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.460
A tyrant stands in a field of
grain and lops off all the top

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:04.830
higher heads of grain.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.000
And that point, you
can't have friendship,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.970
you can't have philosophy,
you can't have faith,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.460
because those are
all things that

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.380
look both closer
inside the human being,

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.970
not just to the tyrant, and
look above the tyrant, too.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.690
And Hitler and Stalin--

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:30.090
just like the great Greek
tyrants and the Roman ones--

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:36.270
they suppress everything
private and everything high.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:40.950
Except now what changes is the
scale is so much better bigger

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:43.290
and it becomes plausible
that you could actually

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.010
remake the world and then
the use of technology

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.580
makes it ever more
potentially intense.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.660
About 60 million people were
killed in the war, about half

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:57.780
of them soldiers, sailors,
and airmen, and about half

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.870
of them civilians.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:06.240
That's three times as much
is the First World War.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.710
The body count is just massive.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:13.020
There are big reasons why the
war is so severe for civilians,

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:18.090
first of all, because technology
and accumulated wealth

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.500
make it possible to wage
a more total kind of war

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.790
even than was waged in the First
World War, which was incredibly

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:29.400
terrible and unprecedented.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.440
And then the second
thing is there

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:37.560
was a mass-produced, organized
effort to murder people,

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.540
whole categories of people.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:44.130
Whole races of people,
whole professions of people.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.590
Nobody had ever seen
anything like that before.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:51.700
There's an organized
attempt at mass slaughter.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.930
That's totalitarianism.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.610
One of the most articulate, and
to my mind, finest human beings

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.590
who ever left was
Winston Churchill,

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.070
who was a friend of freedom.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.970
Parliamentary democracy,
freedom under a Constitution,

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:10.640
Freedom with restraint.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.700
Every person, he loved to quote
can live by no man's leave

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:21.380
beneath the law however
humble that person is born.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.110
The government is to be
accountable to the people.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.740
Churchill said that
there's always two options.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.050
Are the people going to
belong to the government

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.870
or is the government going
to belong to the people?

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.180
He just repeated
that all his life,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.690
and he thought that these great
totalitarian tyrannies, which

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:43.020
he thought that the extremist
movements in his own country,

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.680
including the socialist party
that he opposed all his life

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.790
and was beaten by in
1945, echoed in a milder

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:53.270
form the same thing as these
tyrannies, because they, too,

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:57.380
had this sort of notion that we
can perfect the society if we

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.040
just assemble more power
in the people who lead it.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.650
So it is a great
contest, and it's

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.610
confused between these two
ways of looking at the world.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.090
And they really do
amount to whether you

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.480
have a picture of the human soul
that it's free and therefore

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.770
entitled to govern itself,
which means never to be governed

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.020
except with its consent.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.960
Different than from horses
and dogs and all the things

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:31.090
that we use for pets or this
view that, no, in the end,

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:34.120
we're collective being
shaped by history

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:36.550
and now we're going
to appoint some of us

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.810
to get control of the
effort to shape history.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:47.670
And so the stakes are the
stakes of human freedom, which

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:51.000
depends upon a view
of human nature

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.210
and the government that's
appropriate to that.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.100
And that battle still goes on.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.896
